## Context

The 37 skill bodies mix durable workflow with Claude frontmatter, `/skill` calls, `Skill(...)`
syntax, Claude agent parameters and hard-coded harness paths. Copying them per provider would
fork the most valuable part of IDH. A portable source format must preserve prose and allow
small provider overlays.

## Relevant files

- `skills/*/SKILL.md` — canonical workflows today
- `scripts/skill_frontmatter.py` — existing parser
- `scripts/gen_skills_catalog.py` — skill discovery and catalog generation
- `skills/raid/SKILL.md` — high-coupling orchestration example
- `skills/perch/SKILL.md` — low-coupling instruction-only example

## Actions

1. Define portable frontmatter and semantic references such as `skill:hunt`, `role:coder`,
   and `capability:worktree-isolation`.
2. Define provider overlays only for syntax or behavior the core cannot express.
3. Convert four archetypes first: instruction-only, script-backed, orchestrating, and
   external-service skill.
4. Generate native invocation names and validate every cross-skill reference.
5. Classify the remaining skills into bounded migration waves; do not convert all 37 in one PR.

## Test

- Golden-render the four archetypes for Claude and Codex; deliberately break a semantic
  reference and assert generation fails with source location.

## Verification

- [ ] Portable schema has no provider model or invocation syntax
- [ ] Four archetypes preserve their behavioral contracts
- [ ] Every existing skill is assigned to a migration wave
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- Skill prose has one canonical source.
- Portable Markdown remains canonical; do not introduce a workflow DSL unless the
  four-archetype experiment demonstrates a concrete expression failure.
- Existing Claude invocation names remain stable.
- Provider overlays stay smaller than and subordinate to the portable body.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Portable skill schema and reference resolver are implemented
- [ ] Four representative skills render and smoke-test on both adapters
- [ ] Follow-up migration waves are sized and dependency-ordered
